WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Why does your heart slow again after you stop climbing?

2
00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.740
At the top, your muscles relax, but your heartbeat stays fast.

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.180
A marker sits above its usual band.

4
00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.580
Something must move it back without locking it to one value.

5
00:00:19.033 --> 00:00:22.253
That shaded band is a range, not a single target.

6
00:00:23.733 --> 00:00:25.833
Inside it, the marker can rise and fall.

7
00:00:27.273 --> 00:00:31.893
Homeostasis means keeping internal conditions within a stable range while the

8
00:00:31.893 --> 00:00:36.373
outside changes. The motion matters more than any one reading.

9
00:00:37.483 --> 00:00:38.843
Now warm the body.

10
00:00:40.143 --> 00:00:43.723
The red disturbance pushes the temperature marker above its band.

11
00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:47.643
Sensors in the skin and brain detect that change.

12
00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:51.743
They send blue signals to a control center in the brain.

13
00:00:51.743 --> 00:00:56.503
The control center sends a yellow command toward sweat glands.

14
00:00:57.633 --> 00:00:59.093
Sweat appears on the skin.

15
00:01:00.373 --> 00:01:04.773
As it evaporates, heat leaves the skin, and the marker moves toward the band.

16
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:08.913
The response acts against the warming that started it.

17
00:01:09.833 --> 00:01:12.133
That direction makes this negative feedback.

18
00:01:13.317 --> 00:01:18.337
When internal conditions drift, opposing responses move them toward their range.

19
00:01:19.537 --> 00:01:22.157
The condition can still vary inside that range.

20
00:01:23.857 --> 00:01:25.377
Negative does not mean harmful here.

21
00:01:26.637 --> 00:01:29.397
It means the response pushes against the starting change.

22
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:34.790
Predict what happens if sweat reaches the skin but cannot evaporate.

23
00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.850
Does the marker return as quickly, or does it stay higher?

24
00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:43.070
Keep the body, range, and sweat in view while you decide.

25
00:01:49.083 --> 00:01:52.763
Without evaporation, sweating removes less heat from the skin.

26
00:01:54.023 --> 00:01:57.203
The cooling response is weaker, so the marker stays higher.

27
00:01:58.463 --> 00:02:03.363
The response exists, but its effect depends on what happens after the effector acts.

28
00:02:04.467 --> 00:02:09.527
We can map this loop as change, sensor, control center, effector, and opposing

29
00:02:09.527 --> 00:02:13.347
response. Sweat glands are the effectors here.

30
00:02:14.607 --> 00:02:17.947
Other body loops may combine parts or spread them across organs.

31
00:02:19.287 --> 00:02:22.687
The map is useful, but it is not anatomy for every case.

32
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:28.100
A house thermostat follows a similar path: sense, compare, act.

33
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:35.660
A body is richer. Many sensors and effectors work together, and most responses are

34
00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:39.120
graded. No one sets a dial inside you.

35
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:42.023
A correction also takes time.

36
00:02:43.343 --> 00:02:45.943
Heart rate does not snap to rest when climbing ends.

37
00:02:47.303 --> 00:02:50.163
Signals change, and the rate falls fastest at first.

38
00:02:51.463 --> 00:02:54.063
Then it approaches its resting range more gradually.

39
00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:59.073
Use the simulation to test this map one step at a time.

40
00:03:00.273 --> 00:03:03.853
Then a second film compares cases beyond this complete loop.
